{"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "schema", "document", "Document"], "kwargs": {"metadata": {"source": "data\\documents\\doc\\psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "filename": "psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "filepath": "data\\documents\\doc\\psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "page": 1, "title": "psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "year": "2026"}, "page_content": "Stretch Marks:\u00a0Can occur with long-term use of topical steroids.\u00a0\n\nIncreased Sensitivity:\u00a0Some treatments can make the skin more sensitive to sunlight.\n\nAccessing Topical Treatments in Australia\n\nTopical treatments for psoriasis are widely available across Australia. Over-the-counter options can be purchased from pharmacies, while prescription treatments require a consultation with a healthcare provider. Some treatments may be covered under the Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme (PBS), making them more affordable for patients.\n\nWhen to See a Doctor\n\nIf you notice any adverse reactions or if your symptoms do not improve after several weeks of treatment, it\u2019s important to consult a healthcare professional. They may adjust your treatment plan or explore other options such as systemic therapies or biologics.\n\n\u00a0\n\nPsoriasis Australia provides the information on this website for information purposes only and it does not constitute medical advice. We recommend seeking medical advice from a trained health professional specific to your needs.\n\nPhototherapay", "type": "Document"}}